"Shows numeric magnitudes at geographic locations by scaling symbol area according to the represented quantity.\n\nUse when: Compare totals associated with cities, facilities or geographic aggregates. Avoid coloring entire administrative regions for a count variable.\n\nAvoid when: The symbols would obscure the essential geography or exact spatial distribution inside regions matters.\n\nVisual grammar: Symbol centers identify locations or declared aggregate reference points. Symbol area, rather than radius, scales with the value. A size legend shows reference quantities and any minimum-size adjustment.\n\nBuild: Choose a comparable count or magnitude and a location convention. Calculate symbol sizes using the declared area scaling. Order or separate dense symbols into focused views without moving their geography silently.\n\nCheck: Area scaling matches the numeric values. Aggregate centroids are not interpreted as exact event locations. Use layers or separate views when symbol overlap hides important observations.\n\nPitfalls: Scaling circle radius directly to value and exaggerating differences. Adding a minimum size without disclosing its effect on proportionality.\n\nExample: A library network map places area-scaled circles at branches to compare annual visits while retaining a quiet reference basemap.\n\nAlternatives: Dot-density map, Cartogram."
